WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:06.430
In Japanese culture, anime is a revered artistic
medium.

00:00:06.430 --> 00:00:12.660
Enjoyed by men and women, young and old, and
more recently on every continent—anime transcends

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:18.759
cultural boundaries and can be used as a vehicle
to tell any kind of story imaginable.

00:00:18.759 --> 00:00:24.220
Great anime aren’t just quality entertainment;
they can also offer cutting social commentary,

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:27.189
philosophical musings, and much, much more.

00:00:27.189 --> 00:00:32.130
Not your grandfather’s Saturday morning
cartoons, to be sure—thanks to Japanese

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:37.910
artists are determined to show you just how
thematically deep the realm of animation can

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:38.910
be.

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:47.590
Let’s take a look at some interesting facts
you might not already know about anime.

00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:53.920
Around 60% of the world’s cartoon-animation
entertainment is Japanese-produced anime.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:01:00.240
The first wildly popular anime was Osamu Tezuka’s
Astro Boy in 1963.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:04.439
Astro Boy was also the first animated television
series in Japan.

00:01:04.439 --> 00:01:09.610
Tezuka was greatly influenced by Disney’s
work during the golden age of animation, as

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:11.900
were many of Tezuka’s contemporaries.

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:17.610
He followed up with another hit, Kimba the
White Lion—which, in turn, is seen by many

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:20.990
to have inspired the Disney classic The Lion
King.

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:23.040
Inspiration coming full circle.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:27.700
Early examples of Japanese animation in the
20th century were inspired by animators in

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:33.460
America and Europe, and most were adaptations
of Asian folklore and legends in traditional

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:35.200
Japanese art styles.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:40.710
Science-fiction based mecha anime, also known
as “giant robot” anime, was one of the

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:45.390
first genres to really catch on with viewers
during the anime boom of the 70’s.

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:50.830
Foundational mecha shows like Mobile Suit
Gundam paved the way for the modern anime

00:01:50.830 --> 00:01:54.140
industry, and blazed a broad trail for others
to follow.

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:59.820
Though anime has a reputation of not catering
to Western sensibilities regarding violence

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:06.310
and sexuality, the mid 90’s series Neon
Genesis Evangelion certainly tested the limits

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:09.319
of what was considered acceptable.

00:02:09.319 --> 00:02:15.250
Due to the often shocking and controversial
content of Evangelion, TV Tokyo began heavily

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:21.310
censoring subsequent anime like the acclaimed
Cowboy Bebop, resulting in only half of the

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:25.030
episodes produced for the space western ever
airing on television.

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:30.720
Akira is widely considered to be one of the
most influential works of anime ever created,

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:35.460
introducing anime to the western world when
it burst onto the scene in 1988.

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:40.739
The sci-fi spectacular is said to have been
a strong source for the filmmakers behind

00:02:40.739 --> 00:02:45.440
modern sci-fi films like The Matrix, Chronicle,
and Looper.

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:51.120
The 1995 cyberpunk hit Ghost in the Shell
is another visionary anime which inspired

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:52.620
artists around the world.

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:58.099
The Wachowskis drew upon Ghost in the Shell
as well during development of The Matrix,

00:02:58.099 --> 00:03:03.500
while James Cameron and Steven Spielberg are
both outspoken fans of the anime.

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:10.090
Many anime begin life as manga—a popular
form of printed media in Japan which shares

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:14.600
many similarities with the Western comic book,
though in Japan manga doesn’t suffer from

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:18.720
the same stigma and is enjoyed by people of
all ages.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:25.130
After an anime gains enough popularity, feature
films and videogames typically follow as well.

00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:30.680
In Japan more paper is used to print manga,
than to make toilet paper.

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:36.260
Hiromu Arakawa’s Fullmetal Alchemist is
one prominent example of a manga which spawned

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:41.870
an anime due to its popularity, though it
bears the distinction of having two fully

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:46.530
realized and separate anime adaptations of
the story that don’t actually agree on what

00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:48.280
the ending should be.

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:53.521
The first anime began production while Arakawa
was still hard at work on the manga, and it

00:03:53.521 --> 00:03:56.040
caught up to her before she finished.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:04:00.730
With her approval, it diverged midway through
the story and followed the anime’s unique

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:02.989
plot threads to their own conclusion.

00:04:02.989 --> 00:04:08.890
The second anime, Fullmetal Alchemist: Brotherhood,
began production during the final chapters

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:13.409
of the manga and faithfully adapted the source
material all the way to the end.

00:04:13.409 --> 00:04:20.510
In an unusual turn of events, the wildly popular
Pokemon franchise first appeared on the scene

00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:25.350
as a videogame, with the manga and anime following
afterward.

00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:30.680
Many episodes of the Pokemon anime have been
banned in countries other than Japan due to

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:36.060
content, one particularly infamous episode
has been removed from circulation worldwide

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:38.830
after causing hundreds of seizures.

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:43.740
The episode, named “Electric Soldier Porygon”,
has been recognized by the Guinness Book of

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:49.710
World Records for the “Most Photosensitive
Epileptic Seizures Caused by a TV Show”.

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:55.180
The Dragon Ball franchise has gone both ways
between manga and anime, with the Dragon Ball

00:04:55.180 --> 00:05:01.620
and Dragon Ball Z anime being adapted from
the original manga, while Dragon Ball GT and

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:06.870
Dragon Ball Super were fit to the printed
page after their anime had aired.

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:12.080
It is common for fans of anime and manga in
Western countries to refer to themselves as

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:16.919
“otaku”—a Japanese word for people who
are wholly consumed by their interests.

00:05:16.919 --> 00:05:21.919
Though at a glance it might appear similar
to the relatively mild English words “geek”

00:05:21.919 --> 00:05:27.220
and “nerd”, “otaku” is in fact viewed
by the Japanese as a sort of “warning”

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:32.480
for individuals who obsess over such things
to an unhealthy degree and deserve their often

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:36.360
self-induced ostracization from society.

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:41.350
Expect some disgusted looks if you announce
yourself as an “otaku” in front of someone

00:05:41.350 --> 00:05:43.479
born and raised in Japan.

00:05:43.479 --> 00:05:48.940
Many anime receive a localized “dub” when
they are released internationally, with re-recorded

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:50.670
dialogue for the new regions.

00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:56.140
But this is no small feat—in most cases,
the script writers must meticulously craft

00:05:56.140 --> 00:06:00.330
dialogue that matches up with the existing
lip movements of the characters on-screen,

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:03.360
as the animation work is already complete.

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:09.500
Anime characters are known for their large,
bright eyes and extremely vibrant hair colours.

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:13.370
But they’re eye and hair colours aren’t
just plucked out of the air.

00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:18.240
In Japan colours have great symbolic meaning
and the hair colour of anime characters is

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:22.729
carefully chosen by the animators to reflect
that character’s personality.

00:06:22.729 --> 00:06:28.130
For example, characters with red hair are
typically passionate, feisty, adventurous

00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:30.270
and highly opinionated.

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:34.860
Green hair represents trustworthiness and
tolerance but also jealousy.

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:39.740
White-haired characters are usually mature,
ethereal and magical.

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:45.199
One of the most beloved anime studios around
the world is Studio Ghibli, which has over

00:06:45.199 --> 00:06:51.050
93 production credits spread across three
decades, twenty of which are feature films.

00:06:51.050 --> 00:06:57.380
Hayao Miyazaki and Isao Takahata, Ghibli’s
founders, named the studio after the hot,

00:06:57.380 --> 00:07:02.510
dusty, and often rainy winds which blow across
the Mediterranean from the Sahara; the Arabic

00:07:02.510 --> 00:07:10.009
word for the winds was mispronounced as “Ji-bli”,
and ultimately became the Ghibli we know today.

00:07:10.009 --> 00:07:15.350
Miyazaki has directed many of the studio’s
most successful films, and he has been dubbed

00:07:15.350 --> 00:07:20.540
the “Walt Disney of Japan” due to his
impactful and storied career.

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:26.380
Like Disney, Miyazaki wasn’t afraid to wade
into the nitty gritty work of animated filmmaking.

00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:30.300
During his long and illustrious career with
Ghibli, he took on many different roles to

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:36.290
bring the stories to life; working as animator,
director, producer, storyboard artist, writer,

00:07:36.290 --> 00:07:37.300
and editor.

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:41.910
While directing films, Miyazaki maintained
a “hands on” approach—often personally

00:07:41.910 --> 00:07:47.970
inspecting the key animation and redrawing
it when it didn’t meet his standards.

00:07:47.970 --> 00:07:53.220
The famed director also laments the state
of the modern anime industry, noting that

00:07:53.220 --> 00:07:58.940
many people who work in the thriving industry
“don’t spend time watching real people”

00:07:58.940 --> 00:08:03.949
and “can’t stand looking at other humans”,
which he speculates is because the industry

00:08:03.949 --> 00:08:05.740
is “full of otaku”.

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:10.280
Diehard fans of anime with pipe dreams of
making their own in turn—perhaps he’s

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:11.780
on to something.

00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:14.620
Besides art, another of Miyazaki’s passions
is flight.

00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:18.380
He acquired the interest from his father,
who ran a company that machined parts for

00:08:18.380 --> 00:08:21.949
Japanese aeroplanes during World War II.

00:08:21.949 --> 00:08:26.330
Planes and other flying machines are featured
prominently in Miyazaki’s works.

00:08:26.330 --> 00:08:31.430
Many Ghibli films, like The Secret World of
Arrietty and Pom Poko, are simply masterful

00:08:31.430 --> 00:08:36.319
retellings of older folktales, such as The
Borrowers and Japanese folklore regarding

00:08:36.319 --> 00:08:37.579
the mischievous tanuki spirits respectively.

00:08:37.579 --> 00:08:44.300
As the saying goes: “good artists borrow,
great artists steal”—but the truly great

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:48.339
artists can bring the familiar to life in
exciting new ways.

00:08:48.339 --> 00:08:54.059
Miyazaki’s Spirited Away was the first anime
to even win an Academy Award, taking home

00:08:54.059 --> 00:08:58.009
the gold for Best Animated Feature of 2002.

00:08:58.009 --> 00:09:03.069
Spirited Away is also the highest grossing
Japanese anime film of all time, earning Studio

00:09:03.069 --> 00:09:07.480
Ghibli $330 USD in revenue worldwide.

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:14.110
As animated films began to explore the use
of 3D animation and strong CGI elements, Studio

00:09:14.110 --> 00:09:20.029
Ghibli was determined to continue producing
films using traditional methods of animation—only

00:09:20.029 --> 00:09:23.860
using computer generated imagery to touch
up the hand drawn work.

00:09:23.860 --> 00:09:29.230
Princess Mononoke was one of the first Ghibli
films to feature prominent CGI.

00:09:29.230 --> 00:09:35.930
John Lasseter, CFO of Disney Animation and
Pixar, has fostered an enduring friendship

00:09:35.930 --> 00:09:37.550
with Hayao Miyazaki.

00:09:37.550 --> 00:09:43.019
The two met in 1987 while Lasseter was visiting
Japan, with each having great respect for

00:09:43.019 --> 00:09:44.040
the other’s work.

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:49.139
While not quite a rivalry, they have been
a source of encouragement to one another,

00:09:49.139 --> 00:09:54.600
with John noting that “whenever we get stuck
at Pixar or Disney, I put on a Miyazaki film

00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:57.759
sequence or two, just to get us inspired again”.

00:09:57.759 --> 00:10:03.470
The friendship between Disney and Ghibli goes
even deeper, with a 1996 deal between Tokuma

00:10:03.470 --> 00:10:09.529
Shoten Publishing and The Walt Disney Studios
granting Disney the exclusive honor of serving

00:10:09.529 --> 00:10:12.869
as the international distributor for Ghibli’s
films.

00:10:12.869 --> 00:10:17.389
This includes dubbing the English versions
of the films, a task which Disney takes quite

00:10:17.389 --> 00:10:18.389
seriously.

00:10:18.389 --> 00:10:22.880
Thanks to the House of Mouse, many high profile
actors and actresses have lent their voices

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:25.589
to the English dubs of Ghibli movies.

00:10:25.589 --> 00:10:31.360
Cate Blanchett, John Ratzenberger, Christian
Bale, Matt Damon, Tina Fey, and Liam Neeson—just

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:36.670
to name a few—have all given their own personal
spin to classic Ghibli characters.

00:10:36.670 --> 00:10:42.839
A new species of velvet worm, discovered in
Vietnam in 2013, was given the scientific

00:10:42.839 --> 00:10:50.009
name Eoperipatus totoros as a nod to Studio
Ghibli’s My Neighbor Totoro, due to the

00:10:50.009 --> 00:10:54.570
worm and the Catbus appearing visually similar
because of their many legs.

00:10:54.570 --> 00:10:59.670
Located just outside of Tokyo in the suburb
of Mitaka, there is a museum dedicated to

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:05.619
all things Ghibli, where visitors can meet
an almost life-size Totoro besides other popular

00:11:05.619 --> 00:11:07.819
Ghibli characters and exhibits.

00:11:07.819 --> 00:11:12.670
Hayao Miyazaki, speaking as the museum’s
executive director, hopes to create a place

00:11:12.670 --> 00:11:16.420
“that is interesting”, and “which relaxes
the soul”.

00:11:16.420 --> 00:11:21.999
Somewhere that “those seeking enjoyment
can enjoy, those seeking to ponder can ponder,

00:11:21.999 --> 00:11:28.550
and those seeking to feel can feel”—echoing
sentiments felt in every frame of his films.

00:11:28.550 --> 00:11:33.139
Though Miyazaki has claimed to have retired
several times during his career he announced

00:11:33.139 --> 00:11:39.269
yet another retirement in 2013, stating that
“this time I am quite serious”, citing

00:11:39.269 --> 00:11:41.600
age-related issues with the workload.

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:47.170
Whether he returns to create more masterpieces
or simply enjoys his retirement remains to

00:11:47.170 --> 00:12:13.099
be seen, but no matter his decision, he has
certainly earned his place in history.

